claimed: intent-consumer-seams-a — the two smallest of the seven consumer requests, taken first because one of them misleads a reader today. (6) NAME THE DEPENDENCY, NOT THE DEPLOYMENT: the bake-off tables say "needs a server", which is an assumption about how a caller is deployed rather than a property of a tier; the dependency is String => Embedding, and with an in-process encoder the probe at 88.3% is the CHEAP option rather than the expensive one -- as written the tables argue for the wrong tier. (3) HAND BACK THE RANKING: Probe.Verdict carries margin and runnerUp, NoModel.Verdict keeps best and discards both, so an abstention tells a caller only THAT it declined; the two candidates it could not separate are already computed one layer down and are thrown away on the way out. Wanted by an interface that shows a person the choice, and by active learning, which selects on uncertainty and needs the distribution rather than the winner.
